(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-muscle-oian-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Muscle Origin, Insertion, Action & Innervation Set 2 — Anatomy Exam 4","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which nerve innervates the rhomboid major and minor?","choices":["Long thoracic nerve","Accessory nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"The rhomboids are supplied by the dorsal scapular nerve (C5). — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"Which nerve innervates the flexor carpi ULNARIS?","choices":["Ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"The flexor carpi ulnaris is one of the few anterior forearm muscles supplied by the ulnar nerve (C8–T1). — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"Which nerve innervates the flexor carpi radialis and palmaris longus?","choices":["Ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Deep radial nerve","Axillary nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The flexor carpi radialis and palmaris longus (most anterior forearm flexors) are supplied by the median nerve. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"The posterior forearm extensors (e.g., extensor digitorum, extensor carpi ulnaris) are supplied mainly by which nerve?","choices":["The median nerve","The ulnar nerve","The (deep) radial nerve","The musculocutaneous nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The posterior forearm extensors are supplied by the radial nerve (largely its deep branch). — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"The adductor group of the thigh (e.g., adductor longus, gracilis) is supplied mainly by which nerve?","choices":["The femoral nerve","The sciatic nerve","The obturator nerve","The superior gluteal nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The adductor group (adductor longus/brevis/magnus, gracilis) is supplied mainly by the obturator nerve. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"Which nerve innervates the quadriceps femoris and sartorius?","choices":["The obturator nerve","The sciatic nerve","The femoral nerve","The tibial nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The quadriceps femoris and sartorius (anterior thigh) are supplied by the femoral nerve. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"Which nerve innervates the fibularis (peroneus) longus and brevis?","choices":["The deep fibular nerve","The tibial nerve","The femoral nerve","The superficial fibular (peroneal) nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"The fibularis longus and brevis (lateral compartment) are supplied by the superficial fibular nerve. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"Which nerve innervates the tibialis posterior and flexor digitorum longus?","choices":["The deep fibular nerve","The tibial nerve","The superficial fibular nerve","The obturator nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The deep posterior-compartment muscles (tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus) are supplied by the tibial nerve. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"Which muscle is innervated by the musculocutaneous nerve?","choices":["Coracobrachialis","Anconeus","Flexor carpi ulnaris","Deltoid"],"answer":0,"exp":"The coracobrachialis (with biceps brachii and brachialis) is supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"What is the action of the rhomboid major and minor?","choices":["Protraction and upward rotation of the scapula","Adduction and downward rotation of the scapula","Elevation of the scapula","Depression and protraction of the shoulder"],"answer":1,"exp":"The rhomboids adduct (retract) the scapula and rotate it downward. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"Which forearm muscle SUPINATES the forearm and hand?","choices":["Pronator teres","Supinator","Pronator quadratus","Brachialis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The supinator supinates the forearm and hand by laterally rotating the radius. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"What are the actions of the flexor carpi radialis?","choices":["Flexion and abduction at the wrist","Flexion and adduction at the wrist","Extension and adduction at the wrist","Extension and abduction at the wrist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Flexor carpi radialis flexes and abducts (radial deviates) the wrist. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"What is the action of the serratus anterior?","choices":["Adducts and downwardly rotates the scapula","Elevates the scapula","Extends the arm at the shoulder","Protracts the shoulder and upwardly rotates the scapula"],"answer":3,"exp":"Serratus anterior protracts the shoulder and rotates the scapula so the glenoid cavity moves superiorly (upward rotation). — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"What are the actions of the pectoralis major at the shoulder?","choices":["Flexion, adduction, and medial rotation","Extension, abduction, and lateral rotation","Abduction only","Lateral rotation only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pectoralis major flexes, adducts, and medially rotates the arm at the shoulder. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"What is the action of the extensor carpi ulnaris?","choices":["Flexion and adduction at the wrist","Extension and adduction at the wrist","Flexion and abduction at the wrist","Extension and abduction at the wrist"],"answer":1,"exp":"Extensor carpi ulnaris extends and adducts (ulnar deviates) the wrist. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"Which muscle inserts on the FLOOR of the intertubercular sulcus of the humerus?","choices":["Teres major","Pectoralis major","Deltoid","Latissimus dorsi"],"answer":3,"exp":"The latissimus dorsi inserts on the floor of the intertubercular (bicipital) sulcus. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"On which structure does the brachioradialis insert?","choices":["The radial tuberosity","The olecranon of the ulna","The styloid process of the radius","The coronoid process of the ulna"],"answer":2,"exp":"The brachioradialis inserts on the lateral aspect of the styloid process of the radius. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"The flexor carpi ulnaris inserts on the pisiform, hamate, and which other structure?","choices":["The base of the 5th metacarpal","The base of the 2nd metacarpal","The base of the 3rd metacarpal","The palmar aponeurosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"The flexor carpi ulnaris inserts on the pisiform, hamate, and base of the 5th metacarpal. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"The quadriceps femoris inserts on the tibial tuberosity via which structure?","choices":["The calcaneal (Achilles) tendon","The iliotibial tract","The pes anserinus","The patellar ligament (via the quadriceps tendon and patella)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The quadriceps inserts on the tibial tuberosity through the quadriceps tendon, patella, and patellar ligament. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"The fibularis (peroneus) longus inserts on the medial cuneiform and which other bone (crossing under the foot)?","choices":["The base of the 5th metatarsal","The base of the 1st metatarsal","The calcaneus","The navicular"],"answer":1,"exp":"Fibularis longus crosses the sole to insert on the base of the 1st metatarsal and the medial cuneiform. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"On which structure does the pectoralis minor insert?","choices":["The greater tubercle of the humerus","The lesser tubercle of the humerus","The deltoid tuberosity of the humerus","The coracoid process of the scapula"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pectoralis minor inserts on the coracoid process of the scapula. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"The diaphragm originates from the xiphoid process, ribs 7–12, and which region of the spine (inserting on the central tendon)?","choices":["The cervical spine","The lumbar spine","The sacrum","The manubrium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Per the lecture, the diaphragm arises from the xiphoid, ribs 7–12 and their costal cartilages, and the anterior lumbar spine, inserting on the central tendon. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"The long head of the biceps brachii originates from which bone?","choices":["The humerus","The radius","The scapula","The ulna"],"answer":2,"exp":"Per the lecture, the long head of the biceps brachii originates on the scapula (and inserts on the radial tuberosity). — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"The long head of the triceps brachii originates from which landmark of the scapula?","choices":["The supraglenoid tubercle","The infraglenoid tubercle","The coracoid process","The acromion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Per the lecture, the long head of the triceps arises from the infraglenoid tubercle of the scapula and inserts on the olecranon. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"The external intercostals run from the inferior border of one rib to which border of the rib below?","choices":["The superior border","The inferior border","The costal groove","The costal cartilage"],"answer":0,"exp":"External intercostals run from the inferior border of a rib to the superior border of the rib below, elevating the ribs. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"The hamstrings (including the biceps femoris long head) originate from which pelvic landmark?","choices":["The ischial tuberosity","The anterior superior iliac spine","The pubis","The greater trochanter"],"answer":0,"exp":"The hamstrings originate from the ischial tuberosity (the 'sit bone'). — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which nerve(s) innervate the pectoralis major?","choices":["The long thoracic nerve","The thoracodorsal nerve","The pectoral nerves (medial and lateral)","The axillary nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pectoralis major is supplied by the pectoral (medial and lateral) nerves. — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"What is the shared action of the pronator teres and pronator quadratus?","choices":["Pronation of the forearm","Supination of the forearm","Flexion at the elbow only","Extension at the wrist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Both the pronator teres and pronator quadratus pronate the forearm (medial rotation of the radius). — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"The flexor digitorum profundus inserts on which structures?","choices":["The bases of the middle phalanges of digits 2–5","The base of the distal phalanx of the thumb","The bases of the distal phalanges of digits 2–5","The palmar aponeurosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Flexor digitorum profundus inserts on the bases of the distal phalanges of digits 2–5 (flexing the distal interphalangeal joints). — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"},{"q":"What are the actions of the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus?","choices":["Extension and lateral rotation at the hip","Adduction at the hip","Flexion at the knee","Abduction and medial rotation at the hip"],"answer":3,"exp":"The gluteus medius and minimus abduct and medially rotate the hip (stabilizing the pelvis in gait). — Appendicular Musculature — Muscle Reference (Action, Insertion & Innervation)"}]};
